A||||20||85ZainoGT||fog lights||||6||
Z||000000||85ZainoGT||10-14-2001||02:53 PM||||ok, so for all you fog light people, I am looking to install some fogs on my 85gt.. what is the best type, where do I place them, and how hard is it to get it wired and set up? thanks!<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000001||rogergarrison||10-14-2001||03:53 PM||||Unless you get lots of fog where you live, Id go with driving lights instead. I think Pila (?) or Hella are the best. Pretty simple to hook up. Wiring,switch and directions are usually included. Just please dont drive around with them on all the time, looks so gay  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||85ZainoGT||10-14-2001||04:46 PM||||ya, maybe driving lights is what I am thinking.. thanks...  I can go to any auto shop and find them, right?  still does anyone have the best place to put them on your car, and the best brand.. prices??<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||10-14-2001||06:19 PM||||I got a pair of small, round Blazer projector beam type fog lights from Wal-Mart.  They fit nicely in the front opening of a GT nose, and the bracket they come with is placed perfectly to mount them on the metal cross brace that is just behind the opening.  Mounting them under the nose is asking to break them, especially since mine is lowered.  They use standard H3 halogen bulbs, which are 55 Watts a piece, so they actually put some light on the road, as opposed the "poser/show" types that are so common on most new cars. <BR> <BR>Since they are 110 watts for the pair, they will draw close to 10 amps together, so make a simple LVR relay-type circuit to control them.  This way you can keep the high current wiring ouside of the body, and use a thin wire to control the low current to the relay.  I tapped power directly from the positive post on the battery, with a 15 Amp fuse, to the relay, then up to the lights.  I used 12 gage wire for this.  I used 18 gage wire to tap from the cigarrette lighter light for power to run the relay, since it will only have power if the parking/head lights are on.  Some states require auxilliary lights to be interlocked with the headlights (Virginia, for one, at least when I lived there).  You can also use any accessory power tap, such as the one for the radio.  This way you won't accidentally leave them on.  <P>I installed a plain lighted rocker switch in the dash, under the trunk lid release.  Some people have mounted them in the console.<P>I ran the main power wire under the body along the pass side coolant tube.  The relay is just aft of the battery.  I already have a small hole in the firewall ahead of the battery for my subwoofer power lead, so I used it for the relay power lead.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||85ZainoGT||10-14-2001||06:26 PM||||thanks for all that info, it will come in handy, how much did IT ALL cost you?||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||10-14-2001||06:42 PM||||Lights were $50, relay was about $5, wire and fuses around $10, plus numerous crimp splice connectors.  Switch about $3.<BR>Some light sets come with a relay, wires and a switch.  I didn't like the cheesy toggle switch that came with mine, and the wires that came with the set weren't long enough. Don't remember if the relay was included or not.  <BR>I'm a submarine electrician, so I tend to over build a bit, but it's about as bullet-proof a setup as it gets.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||10-14-2001||06:54 PM||||I removed my switch to my rear window defogger. I dont know why Pontiac even put them in, engine heat works 10 times better. Installed my switch there for my driving lites to come on only when my ignition is on, wiring is already there from defogger, and the defogger wiring has higher amperage rating than the lites. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||SLYGUY||10-15-2001||12:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2birds:<BR><B>Lights were $50, relay was about $5, wire and fuses around $10, plus numerous crimp splice connectors.  Switch about $3.<BR>Some light sets come with a relay, wires and a switch.  I didn't like the cheesy toggle switch that came with mine, and the wires that came with the set weren't long enough. Don't remember if the relay was included or not.  <BR>I'm a submarine electrician, so I tend to over build a bit, but it's about as bullet-proof a setup as it gets.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I want to put some driving lights on my GT also, but I can tell you right now that I'm not gonna put THAT much work into it!!<BR>Glad it works for you, and will last! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000008||Banzai||10-15-2001||07:33 AM||||I grabbed a set of the Hella round projector lamps from The Fiero Store for $20.  They  have metal housings, come with the relay and wiring fuses Etc. The switch is a little weird(under dash mount) but it is backlit. For the price they're hard to beat.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000009||falconhulk||10-15-2001||08:52 AM||||I installed a set of streetlooks neo blue foglights.  The kit comes with a prewired harness with weather prrof connectors.  I have installed a lot of lights but these were the easiest!  They also look awesome on and off (during the day).  All the electrical is done inside the car and you can just wire the hot wire into the back of the large empty slot on the bottom left of the fuse block.  Its ignition switched so they go off when you shut the car off.||||reg||1
Z||000010||85ZainoGT||10-15-2001||12:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Banzai:<BR><B>I grabbed a set of the Hella round projector lamps from The Fiero Store for $20.  They  have metal housings, come with the relay and wiring fuses Etc. The switch is a little weird(under dash mount) but it is backlit. For the price they're hard to beat.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>how hard is it to install as far as wiring and all?<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||gargoyle||10-15-2001||01:45 PM||||Whatever lights you decide on I would recommend that you don't follow all of 2birds advise. Never, ever use those stupid and cheap crimp splices    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">!! Always solder and heat shrink your joints.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> You will be asking for trouble eventually if you use crimp splices. They will vibrate loose when you least expect it. I speak from experience from my younger days back in the dark ages. Using a good switch and a relay are very good advise though.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> <BR>  As for lights I highly recommend PIAA. They have a large variety of sizes and styles. They make a real neat pair of HID driving lights that would fit a GT real nice. You'll pay more but they are worth it. Buy online if possible, you will get your best deal there.  I forgot to mention that with the PIAA lights you will get everything you need, lights, relay, switch, and wiring harness. Just plug & play or light up the road in this case.<P>[This message has been edited by gargoyle (edited 10-15-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by gargoyle (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000012||falconhulk||10-15-2001||03:10 PM||||Crimp splices are fine if you do it right!  Splice them and then tape the end of the splice to thte wire on both sides.  I can do a crimp/tape splice so tight you cant pull it apart.  I have a car thats had them for 5 years without one problem.  I also have a mustang with thme in a HOT engine bay and pleanty of vibration.  Never had one come loose yet.||||reg||1
Z||000013||85ZainoGT||10-15-2001||04:46 PM||||The fiero store has a control kit for the fog lamps, why would I need this?<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Banzai||10-15-2001||05:03 PM||||I haven't installed the lights yet. I'm in the middle of a complete mechanical resto.  They are on a shelf with the other "inspirational parts"- i.e.- 5" monster Tach, new CD receiver, shift knob for shorty conv., Accel 300+ ignition.---------- For any driving light setup you will need a relay, switch, power and ground. The lights I got came with it all. Any that you choose will have instructions. Pretty straight forward. <P>Banzai !!!!!!!<p>[This message has been edited by Banzai (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mickey_Moose||10-15-2001||06:30 PM||||best install I seen is one that removed the original turn signals and install a set of driving/park/turn light that were picked up at Wal-Mart.<P>sorry, too lazy to read how to post pictures here...but if you go to <A HREF="http://www.edmontonfieros.com," TARGET=_blank>www.edmontonfieros.com,</A>  click on the Oct 10 'photo' link - first car second row (click for larger view) - owned by Tony in Calgary<P>anyways.||||reg||1
Z||000016||2birds||10-15-2001||09:08 PM||||Crimp connectors, like anything else, are simply a matter of doing it right, then protecting the joint properly from vibration and moisture.  Friction tape or electrical tape work well.  <P>Soldering is great if you have a clue what you are doing.  Not everybody does.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif">  "The bigger the blob, the better the job", doesn't always work.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Whether you crimp or solder, check the continuity of the splices when you are done, or it will burn up from the high resistance connection.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Mickey_Moose||10-15-2001||09:13 PM||||if you crimp you should cover the ends in a conductive grease - prevents rust from forming inside the connector. Best if you can also cover the connection (crimped of solder) with some heat shrink to help seal it.<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||gargoyle||10-16-2001||02:31 PM||||Ok, here's my defense. My background is military aircraft. Started out on Marine RF4s (VMCJ-2) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> and ended up flying as a inflight tech on Navy P3s (VP-90) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. This is why I strongly believe in solder connections. Alright crimps IF done with the correct tools and finished correctly will last but I still think solder is better. One place I wont bend on my opinion is with those 3M crimp splices that you use a pair of pliers on. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif">JUNK! Well that's my nickles worth.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||Oreif||10-16-2001||09:24 PM||||I use a set of blazer projector driving lights mounted just behind the nose and the housing extends into the scoop area. I have them wired with a relay that has a switch next to the cigarette lighter. I also wired the relay control to the parking lights. This way the driving lights can only be turned on with the parking lights. There is no way you can accidently leave them on. As for crimp or solder, I ALWAYS solder and sleeve the connections. The crimps won't last. I have many years of installing stereo's, alarm systems, and other accessories like fog/driving lights. Anytime a crimp was used they failed over time. They will corrode, loosen up, or even short out. Even wrapping them in tape to keep out moisture doesn't help. Especially in the north where you get temperature extremes of cold winters. All metals expand and contract with hot and cold. Eventually the crimps will condensate and start to corrode. Then losen up due to thermal expansion and cause problems.  <P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000020||FieroJoe||10-16-2001||10:24 PM||||If you don't like your rear window defogger like roger says, you can still retain the stock look but use your rear window defogger switch to control your foglamps, which is what I did.<P>My uncle designed me a latching relay circuit that uses 3 small PC board mount relays that will hold themselves closed and turn on the fog lamps when you push the switch in the UP position, and then when you push the switch to the DOWN it will un-latch and turn the lights off.  I also modified the switch so that the little indicator light would come on when the fog lights were turned on, and the panel light comes on at the appropiate time.<P>Now, if I can only find a way to make the icon in the switch the foglamp symbol, instead of the rear window heater.||||reg||1
